micro air vehicles present a very  appealing platform for search and rescue  civil engineering inspection and a  myriad of other task where it would be  difficult or dangerous to send a human  by flying over difficult terrain na vi's  skipped many of the most challenging  aspects of using a ground vehicle to  carry out these missions in environments  where GPS is available many researchers  have developed systems capable of high  level autonomous behaviors the robust  robotics group and the computer science  and artificial intelligence laboratory  at MIT has developed a micro air vehicle  capable of high level autonomous  behaviors and unconstrained GPS denied  environments we have outfitted a  quadrotor helicopter developed in  collaboration with ascending  technologies with a suite of sensors and  algorithms that successively closed the  loop and stabilize the vehicle in  unknown environments using a lightweight  laser scanner we are able to perform  high-resolution scan matching in real  time to estimate the vehicles relative  position these state estimates are  accurate enough that we can accurately  identify and locate a window in the 3d  point cloud generated from the raw laser  scans this narrow window not much wider  than the vehicle highlights the accuracy  and performance of our state estimation  and control algorithms the laser and  stereo camera allows us to perform  obstacle avoidance in 3d the real-time  state sensing and state estimation  algorithms provide us with a very stable  and robust platform on which to build a  suite of high level mapping and planning  capabilities that do not need to take  into account the fast and unstable micro  air vehicle dynamics we have integrated  the quadrotor with a slam solution that  allows us to map unknown environments  the map generated by the slam algorithm  also allows us to perform completely  autonomous exploration of unknown  environments the exploration module  seeks to balance learning the  geometrical extent of the environment  with sweeping known boundaries of the  map for a visual target this process  must also choose trajectories that  ensure the vehicle remains well  localized  this research culminated in our team's  entry into the 2009 auvsi international  aerial robotics competition the mission  was executed completely autonomously the  vehicle had to take off detect and fly  through a window explore an unknown  environment to find a designated control  panel using the onboard color camera our  vehicle completed the fifth mission in  the first year it was held a feat that  had not been achieved in the 19 year  history of the iarc competitions
 

